IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Civil Appeal No. 1324 of 2019
CITATION: (2017) 14 SCC 80
YEAR: 2017

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: Pinaki Chandra Ghose, R.F. Nariman

PARTIES:
Energy Watchdog vs CERC & Ors

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Contract Act, 1872

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 32 Contract Act, Section 56 Contract Act

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
frustration of contract, force majeure, commercial hardship, price fluctuation

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Held that unexpected rise in raw material or import prices causing commercial hardship does not constitute frustration of contract under Section 56.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 32 Contract Act, Section 56 Contract Act.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

